// Current behavior: checks, after 8 s for an 8,192-byte literal; no diagnostic.
// Copying a quoted literal into an owned fixed byte array costs checking
// time that grows with the square of the literal's length, so a program
// cannot carry an embedded table, test vector or asset of ordinary size.
// Sixty-four kilobytes extrapolates to about seven minutes.
//
// Time in proof checking (Psi 05), compiler a666cf4b30d, one run each on a
// Ryzen 9 7950X; about 2.0 s of every figure is the fixed cost of checking
// any program:
//
//   literal bytes   1,024  2,048  4,096  8,192  16,384
//   owned copy      2.0 s  2.3 s  3.5 s  8.1 s  27.4 s
//
// Variants that bound it, both at the fixed 2.0 s for 8,192 bytes: the same
// literal as a borrowed view, `let text: &[u8] = "...";`, and a
// zero-initialised `[u8; 8192]` local with no literal. The cost therefore
// belongs to the exact-length copy of literal bytes into owned storage
// (data_and_literals.md), not to the literal or to the array. Native
// lowering of the same copy is slower still: at b059ca29483 a 1,480-line
// program holding one 12,343-byte literal took 65 s to check and 201 s to
// check, build and run.
//
// Expected after the copy is checked in time linear in its length: checks in
// about the fixed 2 s, and exits 0 when given a `ProgramEntry` binding.
machine main() crashes Trap {
    let text: [u8; 8192] = "";
    transition text[0] == 65 && text[8191] == 65 {
        true -> done()
        _ -> bad()
    }
    machine done() {}
    machine bad() { crash Trap; }
}
